  "query_preview": "..(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 417 AM EST SUN NOV 22 2026\n\nAt the start of the long term period, shortwave ridging will be \ncentered over the Great Plains, with two well defined shortwaves \nwest of this ridge: one initially located over Alberta, and the \nsecond over the Four Corners region. At the surface, associated \nhigh pressure will be located over the Upper Midwest, maintaining \nnortherly to northeasterly winds and mostly clear skies across the\nforecast area through Tuesday evening. A moderate pressure \ngradient will remain in place, keeping winds gustier than typical \non Tuesday. Expect wind gusts to generally peak in the 10-20 mph \nrange on Tuesday afternoon. This will work to maintain a dry air \nmass, with dewpoints struggling to rise above the mid 20s during \nthe day, although high temperatures will only rise into the upper \n40s and low 50s, which should keep minimum RHs above critical \nthresholds (with the lowest afternoon RH values generally in the \n30-40% range). Overnight, flow aloft will become increasingly \nsouthwesterly to southerly, with total column moisture increasing.\nThis increased moisture, in addition to the approach of the next \nsystem, will allow cloud cover to tick up at least a bit \novernight; however, there is still some disagreement regarding how\nquickly these clouds arrive, with higher than normal spread in \nminTs on Tuesday night/Wednesday morning. Although lows are \ncurrently forecast to range from the upper 20s in the coldest \nvalleys to the mid 30s on ridgetops, temperatures could be a few \ndegrees warmer if clouds arrive sooner, with less terrain \ninfluence in this circumstance. \n\nBy Wednesday, another period of active weather is expected as the\nwave initially over Canada dives southeast along the eastern \nperiphery of midlevel ridging centered over the Intermountain \nWest; meanwhile, the southern wave will push east along the Gulf \nCoast, with 500 mb flow becoming nearly entirely southerly over \nthe forecast area by Wednesday afternoon, even as surface winds \nremain easterly. Initially, this will keep surface dewpoints \nmeager, even as precipitable water increases, with LREF mean PW \nvalues in the 0.7 to 0.8 inch range on Wednesday. In fact, some \ndeterministic model runs suggest PW could rise to over 1 inch by \nlate Wednesday into early Thursday, signaling highly anomalous \natmospheric moisture for this time of year. As the northern \nfeature digs south as a closed low, it will interact with the \nsouthern wave, though models are still struggling to agree on how \nthe two will phase. The latest GFS keeps these waves as two \ndistinct closed lows, while the ECMWF favors a dominant northern \nlow with the southern system remaining a negatively tilted open \nwave within more expansive troughing over the Tennessee Valley. \nDespite these differences, confidence is high that a broad region \nof midlevel PVA will provide ample lift for widespread \nprecipitation across the Ohio Valley, overcoming an initially dry \nsurface air mass through strong dynamic forcing and rich moisture \naloft. Accordingly, NBM PoPs currently peak in the 80% range late \nWednesday into early Thursday, although mentionable PoPs linger \ninto early Friday as of now. Although temperatures will be cool, \nwith lows in the 30s and highs in the upper 40s to low 50s during \nthis time, temperatures aloft will be much too warm for any winter\nprecipitation, with 850 mb temperatures remaining well above \nfreezing (in the 4-8C range) and 850 mb flow remaining southerly \nto southeasterly for much of this active period. Thus, \nThanksgiving looks to bring a cool fall rain rather than any \nwinter weather. \n\nModel spread increases significantly by Friday, with \ndisagreements regarding the speed at which the aforementioned \nsystem departs and the synoptic pattern left in its wake. Cluster \nanalysis shows two distinct scenarios: in the first, a more robust\nridge builds in to the west, with shortwave ridging in the \nMidwest and a much less defined trough over the eastern CONUS. In \nthe other scenario, a broader and deeper trough remains over \nroughly the eastern half of the CONUS, with ridging remaining well\nto the west, keeping eastern Kentucky thoroughly within a \nstronger troughing pattern. In both scenarios, a shortwave does \nlook to approach the forecast area later in the day on Saturday, \nwhich could lead to additional rainfall; however, in the absence \nof better model agreement, any further details regarding this \npattern remain sparse. Thus, PoPs are below mentionable thresholds\nfor now.",
